.Dd November 21, 2026
.Dt JOBMARSHAL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jobmarshal
.Nd per-user build coordination service
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm help
.Op Ar command
.Nm
.Cm start
.Op Fl j Ar N | Fl -jobs Ar N
.Nm
.Cm stats
.Op Fl -zero
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm stop
.Nm
.Cm version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages a private per-user service on Linux and FreeBSD.
The
.Cm help
and
.Cm version
commands print command-line information without accessing service configuration.
.Ss Starting the service
.Cm start
starts a detached daemon with its diagnostics in the private
.Pa daemon.log
file in the runtime directory.
It prints a shell-quoted export of
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
on standard output, so a shell can use:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
eval "$(jobmarshal start -j 8)"
.Ed
.Pp
The maximum job capacity comes from
.Fl j
or
.Fl -jobs ,
then
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_JOBS ,
then the number of available CPUs.
The value must be a positive decimal integer.
Starting an existing service succeeds when it is responsive and not draining.
An explicitly requested capacity must match; stop the service before changing it.
.Ss Inspecting and stopping the service
.Cm status
reports the daemon PID, maximum jobs, active and waiting build counts, and draining
state.
.Cm stop
rejects queued and new independent builds, waits for active builds to finish,
then acknowledges the request before complete teardown.
Nested wrappers may finish using their inherited allocation while draining.
Stable lock files remain in the runtime directory after shutdown.
.Ss Statistics
.Cm stats
reports the listening socket, token FIFO, daemon PID, UTC start and reset
timestamps, maximum jobs, available and unavailable tokens, active builds,
processed builds, and draining state.
Timestamps use UTC RFC3339 format.
The command does not start an absent service.
.Pp
Processed builds count admitted leases when they end, including failed commands.
Queued requests that never receive admission do not count.
.Fl -zero
resets the processed count and reset timestamp before printing the snapshot.
It preserves the start timestamp, active builds, and token state.
Builds active during reset count when they subsequently finish.
All statistics remain in memory and restart resets them.
.Pp
Token counts are observations that can change immediately after collection.
Unavailable tokens include implicit build reservations, native token holders,
and tokens lost by native clients; they do not measure processes or threads.
Job demand and wait duration are not measured.
Statistics observe the FIFO without consuming tokens or changing descriptor flags.
.Ss Runtime directory
The runtime directory is selected from
.Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR ,
then the XDG runtime directory with
.Pa jobmarshal
appended, then
.Pa /tmp/jobmarshal- Ns Ar uid
if no usable XDG runtime directory exists.
The final path must be absolute UTF-8 without whitespace.
It must be a real directory owned by the current user without group or other
permissions; a new directory is created with mode 0700.
The service rejects unsafe directories, lock files, and log files.
.Pp
Ordinary control operations have bounded connection and I/O deadlines.
Admission and draining wait for build completion without a fixed deadline.
Missing or refused sockets indicate an absent service; other failures are errors.
.Ss Shared job budget
The private token FIFO holds one token for every configured job slot.
Each independent wrapped build reserves one for its implicit slot; native GNU
make and Cargo clients share the remaining tokens.
Inherited allocations let nested wrappers run without reserving another slot.
Tokens lost by native clients reduce capacity until a safe stop and restart.
Surviving builds prevent replacement of a crashed daemon's pool until they exit.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev JOBMARSHAL_RUNTIME_DIR
Explicit private runtime directory.
.It Ev JOBMARSHAL_JOBS
Explicit capacity used when the command line does not select one.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
XDG runtime base directory, accessed through the XDG library.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Cm help ,
.Cm start ,
.Cm stats ,
.Cm stop ,
and
.Cm version
commands return 0 on success and 1 on execution failure.
.Cm status
returns 0 when the service responds and 1 when absent or on an error.
Usage errors, including a missing command, return 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jobmarshal-wrapper 1
.Pp
